.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_alloc_entry \- allocate an entry from the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_mempool_alloc_entry(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a pointer to a free entry of the pool. When the pool is empty
and the fallback is on, the entry comes from the heap instead. The
contents of the entry are uninitialized.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Memory pool to allocate from (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the allocated entry on success
Returns NULL when the pool is empty and the fallback is off
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
The memory that comes back is uninitialized
The pointer that comes back is aligned for any object type, as with
malloc(), so any object that you can keep in heap memory can go into a
pool entry
The caller must free the entry with ccol_mempool_free_entry()

On a thread-safe pool the entry comes from a small cache of the calling
thread, and the shared free list is reached only when that cache needs
a refill. A pool too small to keep a cache (fewer than 8 elements)
takes the pool's lock on every call instead, and so does a thread that
the pool has no cache left for; that is one lock for each call, as on
a pool without a cache. A refill, and every call that has to go past
the cache, costs one lock. ccol_mempool_create(3) explains how to size
a pool, and also asks you to measure a configuration before you settle
on it.

A NULL mp is a fatal error: the call prints a message and ends the
process with abort(), in every build.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers don't contend on the common path.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_calloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_free_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
